<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich von Wangen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1218">1218</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Federico Vanga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich von Wangen was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> from 1207 to 1218 and an imperial official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He strengthened the bishops' rule over their principality and had their rights recorded in the Codex Wangianus. In 1208 he issued regulations for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> miners of Monte Calisio near Trent, among the earliest mining laws in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1218 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-von-wangen.jpg" alt="Friedrich von Wangen" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_Vanga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
